WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.640
It seems as though "Ted Lasso" has ridden off into&nbsp;
the sunset. If the third-season finale really is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:10.783
the end, did this show touted as a champion of&nbsp;
kindness give its characters a fitting sendoff?

00:00:10.783 --> 00:00:15.000
"Ted Lasso" begins with the title character&nbsp;
moving to England to take a job managing an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.520
underdog English football team, AFC Richmond.&nbsp;
He doesn't know a thing about soccer, but taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:26.033
the job serves as an escape. He and his wife need&nbsp;
some space as they try to repair their marriage.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:34.020
"What, no, what did I say? That's what I'm&nbsp;
doing, though. I'm giving you that space."

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:39.240
Lasso sees the team through brutal losses&nbsp;
and terrific wins, relegation and promotion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:43.740
personal issues and self-discovery. Season&nbsp;
3 sees Ted coming to terms with the end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:47.640
his marriage and making a big decision to&nbsp;
maintain a relationship with his son Henry.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:52.500
The start of the third season also sees "wonder&nbsp;
kid" Nathan Shelley coaching West Ham United.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:57.480
The former Richmond kit man grapples with his&nbsp;
newfound responsibility before quitting and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:02.340
trying to rediscover who he is. Roy Kent also&nbsp;
goes on a journey of self-discovery after a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:07.320
breakup with Keeley, and even Coach Beard finally&nbsp;
spreads his wings and learns to fly on his own.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.620
Several players on the team also&nbsp;
go through personal plights as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:13.980
the season progresses. Colin shares&nbsp;
his sexual orientation with the team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:19.380
Jamie learns how to be a team player, and Sam&nbsp;
opens the Nigerian restaurant of his dreams.

00:01:19.463 --> 00:01:23.520
The apparent series finale sees everyone&nbsp;
coming to terms with Ted's ultimate decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:27.720
to leave the team to be with his family. The&nbsp;
players and a select group of fans perform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:32.820
a coordinated musical number at Ted and Coach&nbsp;
Beard's last practice while Rebecca refuses to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:37.572
acknowledge Ted's decision until push comes&nbsp;
to shove. She threatens to sell the team.

00:01:37.572 --> 00:01:38.668
"If you go, I go."

00:01:38.668 --> 00:01:43.500
AFC Richmond wins a nail-biting final match&nbsp;
against West Ham, but not the Premier League,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.640
leaving them without the final feather in&nbsp;
their cap. After a disappointing first half,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.820
the team reveals they've kept the&nbsp;
pieces of the iconic "Believe" sign,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:54.360
which Nate destroyed back at the end&nbsp;
of Season 2, piecing it back together.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:59.160
Everyone else seems to get a happy ending.&nbsp;
Roy Kent becomes the new Richmond manager&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:03.480
and goes to therapy. Coach Beard stays&nbsp;
behind and marries Jane. Sam gets to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:08.160
play for the Nigerian national team. Rebecca&nbsp;
ends up selling 49% of the team to the fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:12.060
keeping majority ownership, while also having&nbsp;
a chance encounter with the man she met in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:16.260
Amsterdam earlier in the season. Nate finds&nbsp;
happiness with his girlfriend, Jade, and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:21.660
family. Keeley's PR firm is going strong. Jamie&nbsp;
reconnects with his father. Journalist Trent Crimm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:27.146
publishes his book. And Keeley and Rebecca seem&nbsp;
prepared to start an AFC Richmond women's team.

00:02:27.146 --> 00:02:31.560
Throughout the series, Ted does what he can to&nbsp;
make the lives of those around him better. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:35.700
when his marriage fails, it's almost impossible&nbsp;
for him to mentally grasp, because he couldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:40.680
make Michelle's life better or "keep her happy,"&nbsp;
no matter how hard he tried. Season 3 sees Ted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:45.300
learn how to accept what he can't control and&nbsp;
to let it go, especially with help from Rebecca,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:48.540
who went through something similar in&nbsp;
the aftermath of her divorce from Rupert.

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:52.740
Rather than continue to run away from what he&nbsp;
can't control, Ted decides to go back home to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:57.000
Kansas, even if it means he is returning to an&nbsp;
environment that reminds him of his failure.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.620
Acceptance is a significant theme across the&nbsp;
series, particularly in the final season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:06.660
and not just for Ted. Rebecca accepts that there&nbsp;
is nothing she did to cause her ex-husband Rupert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:11.100
to cheat on her with younger women; it's something&nbsp;
he will always do, no matter how young or perfect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:15.480
his wife is. She also has to accept that her&nbsp;
marriage prevented her from doing some things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:19.980
in her life. She goes to a fertility specialist&nbsp;
early in the season and discovers she can no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:24.180
longer have biological children, forcing her to&nbsp;
come to terms with the loss of that possibility.

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:29.340
Season 3 also puts a spotlight on Colin, who's&nbsp;
closeted and worried about coming out to the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:34.320
players on the team. When Trent unknowingly comes&nbsp;
across Colin kissing his partner, he provides a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:39.060
helpful ear as Colin struggles with revealing&nbsp;
his sexuality. Colin's best friend, Isaac,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:43.500
later looks at Colin's texts and finds messages&nbsp;
to his boyfriend. After some initial anger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:47.520
Isaac tries to understand and accept why his&nbsp;
friend didn't come out to him. He eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:51.600
confronts Colin at his home, asking if he had&nbsp;
done something to make his friend think he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:55.834
wouldn't accept him. When Colin's ready,&nbsp;
Isaac and the team readily support him.

00:03:56.015 --> 00:03:59.555
"Who's the fittest guy on the team?"
"I won't tell you, and you could never guess."&nbsp;

00:04:00.643 --> 00:04:03.206
"Bumbercatch."
"Yes."

00:04:03.206 --> 00:04:06.900
While AFC Richmond has always seemed&nbsp;
like a tight-knit group of friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:11.940
there were conflicts. For example, Jamie was a&nbsp;
ball hog on the pitch, often refusing to pass.

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:14.880
With his sunny disposition&nbsp;
and unconventional methods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:19.560
Ted changes that. Over his three years with the&nbsp;
team, he encourages them to be honest and open,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:24.600
to support one another on and off the field, and&nbsp;
to reflect on how they can improve themselves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:28.320
He even gives them each a book that he&nbsp;
thinks will help them on their journeys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:33.180
to self-discovery. By the end of the series, Ted&nbsp;
has cultivated teammates that are fiercely loyal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:37.560
to one another. A prime example from Season 3&nbsp;
is when Sam's restaurant is vandalized after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:41.460
a public argument with a politician. The&nbsp;
team bands together to repair the damage.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:44.100
Ted takes a team and turns them into a family.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:47.995
This is undoubtedly a major&nbsp;
theme of the entire series.

00:04:47.995 --> 00:04:50.820
Men's mental health is often a stigmatized topic,

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:54.360
but "Ted Lasso" doesn't flinch away&nbsp;
from the issue. Characters take the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:58.200
time to reflect on themselves and what they&nbsp;
need to maintain their mental well-being.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:02.580
A prime example is how Roy initially reacts&nbsp;
to Trent's presence during the season. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:06.660
journalist wrote a fairly scathing review&nbsp;
of Roy after his first professional game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:11.820
which has haunted Roy for decades. Not only does&nbsp;
Roy come to accept that it wasn't a personal dig,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:15.720
but he also starts to understand how it&nbsp;
impacted his mindset in his career and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:20.160
personal life. In breaking up with Keeley,&nbsp;
Roy realizes he needs to work on himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:23.520
though he waits until the end of the&nbsp;
season to really take those steps.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:27.900
The season also has what is a sometimes&nbsp;
difficult-to-watch breakdown on Jamie's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:31.980
part. He clearly has a significant amount of&nbsp;
weight on his shoulders as the season progresses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:36.900
especially as the team begins to soar. This&nbsp;
results in a sobbing fit in the locker room&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:41.640
before a key game. Rather than allow himself to&nbsp;
bottle it up, he lets it out in front of Roy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:45.180
who in the past probably would have&nbsp;
ridiculed him for it, and makes a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:50.340
point to go and visit the person in his life&nbsp;
who always makes him feel better: his mom.

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:54.780
The third season of "Ted Lasso" is filled&nbsp;
with turbulence, but everyone lands safely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:58.200
in the end. Many of the characters get&nbsp;
what they've wanted throughout the season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:02.580
whether it be more time with their family or&nbsp;
the chance to play for their dream team. Some,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:07.140
like Roy and Jamie, have what they need rather&nbsp;
than what they want, which is to be with Keeley.

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:11.340
Some critics and viewers may think it's&nbsp;
unrealistic for everyone to be in such good places&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:15.300
in the end, but it reinforces what audiences&nbsp;
have come to know and love about the series:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:19.980
we get what we give back to the world. The&nbsp;
only person in a negative place is Rupert,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:24.180
who, after being the leading antagonist for&nbsp;
the entirety of the series, has fallen from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:27.240
grace after his new ex-wife and&nbsp;
assistant speak out against him.

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:32.100
The people who put in the work are in a better&nbsp;
place now, which is all Ted wanted for them when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:36.600
he met them three years ago. He made a lasting&nbsp;
impact that has helped the people in his life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:40.693
discover new things about themselves,&nbsp;
including what brings them fulfillment.

00:06:40.693 --> 00:06:43.020
"Thank you."
"Oh, I'm sorry."&nbsp;

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:44.742
"No, I'm sorry."
"Please, go first."

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:49.980
The ending of "Ted Lasso" seems to wrap most&nbsp;
things up with a bow. Almost too neatly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:53.700
according to some fans and critics. The&nbsp;
montage of everyone's happy ending plays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:56.940
out on screen only for the next cut&nbsp;
to be Ted waking up on the airplane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.940 --> 00:07:01.140
landing back in the United States for the&nbsp;
first time since leaving for England. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:05.640
has caused fans to question whether the happy&nbsp;
endings we see are actually Ted's dreams.

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:09.180
There is an argument to be made that&nbsp;
what we are seeing is what Ted wants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:13.260
for them and not what actually happens. Ted&nbsp;
isn't present during the wedding sequence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:17.340
which seems odd considering how close&nbsp;
he and Coach Beard are. Wouldn't he fly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:20.160
back to see his best friend get&nbsp;
married in front of Stonehenge?

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:24.240
In a Reddit AMA, Brendan Hunt, who&nbsp;
plays Beard and co-created the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:29.460
had an answer. When asked if the wedding is real&nbsp;
or in Ted's mind, Hunt simply replied, "Real."

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:34.440
But then fans wanted to know why Ted wasn't at&nbsp;
Coach Beard's wedding. Hunt explained that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:38.160
characters' friendship has had periods of&nbsp;
not seeing each other, and he expects that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:42.420
the future we're seeing falls into that. He&nbsp;
also argued the reasoning is "narrative" and,

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:45.780
"It would be anticlimactic and&nbsp;
deflating if we suddenly see him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:48.193
back there before we even see his plane [land]."

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:52.800
The creators and cast of "Ted Lasso" have&nbsp;
been upfront about the future of the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:56.160
or lack thereof. In an interview&nbsp;
with Deadline, Jason Sudeikis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:59.460
who co-created the show and plays&nbsp;
Ted, made it pretty clear, saying ,

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:01.860
"This is the end of this&nbsp;
story that we wanted to tell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:04.334
that we were hoping to tell,&nbsp;
that we loved to tell."

00:08:04.334 --> 00:08:06.660
"There's a lot of things we&nbsp;
don't get in life that we want,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:11.734
but it's the journey to get there. So I have&nbsp;
to get, kind of, done with this chapter."

00:08:11.734 --> 00:08:15.300
Fans have known that the series was designed&nbsp;
for three seasons for quite some time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:19.620
particularly after Brett Goldstein, who&nbsp;
plays Roy Kent and is a writer on the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:23.100
confirmed the designated end&nbsp;
in 2022 to Variety, stating,

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:24.575
"It was planned as three."

00:08:24.575 --> 00:08:28.740
In his Reddit AMA, Brendan Hunt expressed&nbsp;
that nothing is set in stone at the moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:31.740
but the creative team needs to&nbsp;
rest before they talk about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:34.260
future of the series and characters. He explained,

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:38.460
"We don't know. We need a break and will take&nbsp;
one presently. Nothing has been ruled out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:41.100
everything is possible; but that&nbsp;
includes the possibility that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:45.970
we're done. We won't know until we've sat&nbsp;
with it for a while, decompressed, etc."

00:08:45.970 --> 00:08:51.000
Even though the series is likely over, the team&nbsp;
isn't writing off the possibility of developing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:56.160
a spin-off series or two. Sudeikis told Deadline&nbsp;
that spin-offs were still on the table, saying,

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:59.340
"I think that we've set the table&nbsp;
for all sorts of folks ... to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:01.380
to watch the further telling of these stories."

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:04.560
Hunt reaffirmed the chance of&nbsp;
a spin-off in his Reddit AMA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:07.740
confirming that discussions still need&nbsp;
to happen after a well-deserved break.

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:12.180
When asked about the chance of a spin-off&nbsp;
about the AFC Richmond women's team Keeley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:16.500
and Rebecca plan in the finale, Juno Temple,&nbsp;
who plays Keeley, didn't rule it out in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:20.520
interview with Deadline. She noted that the&nbsp;
series showcases female friendships, adding,

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:23.880
"It would be amazing to see how they would&nbsp;
continue to do wonderful things together."

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:26.040
Hannah Waddingham, who plays Rebecca,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:29.160
echoed Temple's thoughts in her own&nbsp;
interview with Deadline, commenting,

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:31.440
"I love the arc of Keeley's character to present&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:33.300
something to Rebecca that&nbsp;
she hasn't thought about."

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:36.060
Also in an interview with Deadline, Phil Dunster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:39.900
who plays Jamie, offered the view that&nbsp;
it's alright if this is the end, saying,

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:44.280
"I would love for there to be more but&nbsp;
also, if that's all she wrote, let it be."

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:48.480
As far as we know right now, the third&nbsp;
season of "Ted Lasso" is indeed the end.

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:53.520
As the end of a series, it provides closure&nbsp;
for most of the storylines fans dug into over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:58.320
the span of 34 episodes. From Ted learning to&nbsp;
accept what he can't change to Roy realizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:02.520
he needs to grow for himself and himself&nbsp;
alone, the team comes out on the other side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:06.840
of their time with Ted with a new perspective&nbsp;
on life and memories that will last forever.

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:12.060
The end of "Ted Lasso" also means the end to some&nbsp;
important on-screen representation of men's mental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:17.040
health, from Ted's panic attacks to Nate's implied&nbsp;
depression after quitting his dream job. In turn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:21.360
the series also shows its characters doing the&nbsp;
work to better themselves by going to therapy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:26.100
something that is still stigmatized in many&nbsp;
places today. Hopefully, "Ted Lasso" will always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:31.620
be available to watch on Apple TV+, helping break&nbsp;
the stigma for new audiences for years to come.

